            <ArticleTitle>[Clinical analysis of efficacy and quality of life of segmental bowel resection for bowel endometriosis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>503-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3760/cma.j.issn.0529-567X.2016.07.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the efficacy and quality of life of segmental bowel resection for bowel endometriosis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Totally 62 symptomatic patients with bowel endometriosis undergoing segmental bowel resection were recruited. A visual analogue scale(VAS)and the 36-item short form health survey(SF-36)questionnaire were administered before and at least 1 year after surgery, respectively. Pregnancy rates were also recorded.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Sixty-two patients in total underwent follow-up ranging from 12 to 74 months. All patients complained of obvious pain symptoms, including dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, pain on defecation and chronic pelvic pain. The relief of dysmenorrhea(2.9 ± 2.2 versus 7.5 ± 2.9), dyspareunia(0.7 ± 0.5 versus 4.3 ± 2.2)and pain on defecation(1.6 ± 0.7 versus 7.3 ± 1.9)after surgery was statistically significant(all P&lt;0.01). The scores for all 8 domains of the SF-36 questionnaire were significant improved after segmental bowel resection(all P&lt;0.01). The complication rate was 45%(28/62), including 18 cases of urinary retention, 4 rectovaginal fistulas, 2 cases of vaginal dehiscence, and 1 case each of thrombogenesis, pelvic abscess and general peritonitis. All of the patients with complications recovered well throughout follow-up. The postoperative pregnancy rate of the previous infertile patients was 6/10. Among the 6 gestational cases, 2 had labour, 2 underwent caesarean sections, one had a spontaneous natural abortion, and one underwent uterine curettage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Segmental bowel resection could significantly relieve pain and improve quality of life for patients with bowel endometriosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
